/**
\page architecture Yoyo Architecture

The architecture for the Yoyo application is meant to be simple and obeying the rule of "high
cohesion - low coupling". This means, that between modules there must be a minimal amount of
relationships.

This was achieved by defining a very slim and basic base module - the SDK - which is also published
to the customer for plugin creation. Almost all other modules depend on it.

\section architecture_responsibility One module - one responsibility

Using the Single Responsibility Principle (S in SOLID), one module is only responsible for one part
of the software. It must cover everything, that is concerned by this part (high cohesion), but not
more.

Communication between modules is minimized (low coupling) and in most cases routed through the
module, that \b must depend on all: the Yoyo application.


\image html architecture_modules.svg "Dependencies of Yoyo modules"

\section archtiecture_sdk Yoyo SDK

Here is an architectureal model for the Yoyo SDK:

\image html architecture_sdk.svg "Class diagram for Yoyo SDK"

\section architecture_datamodel Data model

The Yoyo data model follows some fixed rules.

First of all: the Yoyo data model is a tree structure.

The root node of the tree is always an object of type yoyo::configuration.

The root node always has the three child nodes: yoyo::configuration_info, yoyo::data_root and
yoyo::gui_node. It can have other child nodes of type yoyo::protocol or yoyo::communication. Other
types of child nodes are not allowed.

All mentioned nodes are created by the fundamental node factory, which is not exposed to the Yoyo
plugin framework. These node classes are declared in the datamodel-nodes module.

\image html architecture_datamodel.svg "Data model for a Yoyo configuration"

Except for the yoyo::configuration_info node, all of the child nodes of the root node may have
any number of children, depending on the type of node, different factories are used for node
creation:

- For creating child nodes for yoyo::data_root, the data factory is used.
- For creating child nodes for yoyo::gui_root, the gui factory is used.
- For creating child nodes for yoyo::protocol, the protocol factory is used.
- For creating child nodes for yoyo::communication, the communication factory is used.
*/
